<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">McMahon–Hussein Correspondence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hussein–McMahon Correspondence</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The McMahon–Hussein Correspondence was an exchange of letters in 1915–1916 between Sir Henry McMahon, British High Commissioner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Hussein bin Ali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharif of Mecca</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> promised to recognise Arab independence in a broad region in return for an Arab revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which began in June 1916. Disputes over whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> was included shaped Arab grievances after the war.</p></div>
